use @piton/belay

from ./lib/PitonSkillBase import PitonSkillBase

export skill CruxImplReconcile extends PitonSkillBase:
    description:
        Examines differences between a project Piton specifications
        and its actual implementation, classifies why they diverge,
        and helps reconcile them without assuming either side is
        always correct.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to compare specifications with implementation,
        detect specification-to-implementation drift, check compliance,
        or reconcile intended and actual behavior
    prompt:
        Fluency is read once per context. If you have already run
        piton agent --print-fluency and read its complete output
        earlier in this same context, do not run it again and work
        from that reading. Otherwise, before any Piton work, run it in
        the project and read the complete output, treating it as the
        authoritative reference for Piton syntax, semantics, structure,
        imports, inheritance, composition, analyzer capabilities, and
        validation. Do not infer compliance from raw source-text
        similarity, and if fluency cannot be obtained, report the
        limitation rather than guessing.

        Your central question is where the specification and the
        implementation disagree, and what should be done about it. This
        includes specification-to-implementation drift detection as a
        core responsibility. Do not create or suggest a separate drift
        skill for it. Never assume the specification is always correct
        or the implementation is always correct. A difference may mean
        the implementation is incomplete, the implementation violates a
        requirement, the implementation has undocumented behavior, the
        specification is outdated, the requirements are ambiguous, the
        implementation exposes an unknown constraint, an intentional
        design change was never specified, or existing tests do not
        adequately establish compliance. The goal is a coherent
        relationship between intended and actual behavior.

        Honor any user-supplied scope, which may be the entire project,
        a subsystem, a feature, a module, a particular requirement, a
        specific implementation change, or selected source files.
        Otherwise determine a practical project-wide analysis strategy.
        Never make unsupported claims about parts of the codebase that
        were not inspected.

        Establish the specification baseline before examining behavior.
        Inspect the relevant Piton specifications, resolve their
        imports, inheritance, and composition, and identify the explicit
        behavioral requirements, architectural constraints, invariants
        and failure semantics, relevant acceptance criteria, and existing
        implementation references where available. Use Piton compiler
        and analyzer capabilities where appropriate. Then examine the
        relevant implementation artifacts, which may include source code,
        public APIs, internal interfaces, tests, runtime configuration,
        serialization formats, build definitions, generated code,
        execution traces, logs, test results, benchmarks, and existing
        documentation, and determine what the implementation actually
        guarantees. Never treat comments or function names as conclusive
        evidence of behavior, and never claim a requirement is satisfied
        merely because a corresponding function exists. Where practical,
        use executable tests or targeted experiments to verify important
        claims. Distinguish static code evidence from observed runtime
        behavior, and state the limitation where tests cannot be
        executed.

        Map specified capabilities and behavioral requirements to the
        relevant implementation components, the evidence of compliance,
        the evidence of noncompliance, the existing tests, and the
        unresolved verification questions. Require no one-to-one
        relationship, because one requirement may span several modules
        and one component may satisfy several requirements. Use actual
        semantic relationships rather than naming similarity alone.

    classifyingDivergence:
        Classify every significant difference into the category the
        evidence supports. A missing implementation is a specified
        capability with no identified implementation, distinguishing
        confirmed absence from insufficient inspection evidence. A
        behavioral contradiction is implementation behavior demonstrably
        different from the specification, such as an operation that
        modifies persistent state before returning the error its
        specification requires to leave inputs unchanged. A partial
        implementation has some required behavior while important cases
        remain unsupported. Undocumented implementation behavior is
        behavior the specification does not address, judged only where it
        is relevant to public contracts, correctness, or architectural
        decisions, since not every implementation detail deserves a
        requirement. An outdated specification is one the implementation
        has intentionally moved beyond, where the authorization of that
        decision must be established before recommending a specification
        update. An ambiguous specification permits multiple
        interpretations that make compliance uncertain. Unverified
        compliance may hold but lacks sufficient evidence, and must
        never be confused with confirmed noncompliance. Structural
        divergence is implementation architecture conflicting with
        established specification constraints or subsystem boundaries,
        judged by whether it causes an actual contract violation.

        Give every significant finding its relevant requirement, its
        affected implementation, the observed or inferred difference,
        its supporting evidence, its confidence, its consequences, and
        its recommended action. Use the confidence classifications
        Confirmed, Strongly supported, Suspected, and Unverified, and
        never present speculative code interpretations as demonstrated
        defects. Where tests establish behavior, explain what was
        actually tested.

        Never automatically choose which side should change. For every
        significant divergence, evaluate changing the implementation,
        which fits when the specification is authoritative and the
        implementation violates an established requirement, changing the
        specification, which fits when an intentional approved decision
        should become formal, and investigating further, which fits when
        requirements are ambiguous, runtime behavior is uncertain, a
        technical constraint is not understood, or neither side is
        confidently authoritative. Explain the reasoning each time.
        Changing the specification in a way that alters established user
        intent requires explicit approval, and a requirement must never
        be weakened automatically merely to accommodate existing code.

        For significant discrepancies, propose an actionable plan naming
        the discrepancy, its evidence, the recommended reconciliation
        direction, the affected specifications and implementation
        components, the relevant tests, the dependencies, the risk, and
        the verification strategy. Order changes to preserve project
        consistency, prioritize high-impact behavioral contradictions
        and correctness violations, and prefer smaller corrections over
        large rewrites where they restore compliance.

        The default operation is diagnostic. Never modify source code or
        specifications merely because a discrepancy was found. When the
        user explicitly authorizes a correction, establish the intended
        behavior, identify the affected artifacts, preserve unrelated
        modifications, apply the smallest coherent change, run the
        appropriate validation, verify specification compliance, and
        report the remaining discrepancies. Respect the distinction
        between specification changes and implementation changes.
        Delegate a specialized subtask to an appropriate installed skill
        only optionally, such as crux-spec-review for contradictory
        requirements, crux-spec-deep-dive for needed user decisions, or
        crux-spec-develop for revising a specification, and never require
        another skill.

        Present an overall alignment summary without unsupported
        completeness percentages, the confirmed discrepancies with
        references to both specification and implementation evidence, the
        suspected or unverified discrepancies kept separate, the
        recommended reconciliation for each important finding as change
        the implementation, change the specification, or investigate,
        the verification gaps where compliance cannot currently be
        established, and the single highest-value next correction or
        investigation. Never bury a critical correctness violation beneath
        minor differences.

        Support repeated reconciliation as the project evolves, since
        drift detection happens when this skill is invoked, with no
        daemon or background monitoring. When previous results are
        available, distinguish previously detected issues now resolved,
        persisting discrepancies, newly introduced drift, and previously
        unverified requirements now supported by evidence, re-evaluating
        everything against the current project and never assuming a prior
        discrepancy remains valid.
